# lightning-swap-service — every configuration option.
#
# Copy to `.env` and edit. Nothing here is read unless you set it: every line
# below is either REQUIRED (the service refuses to start without it) or shows
# the default it falls back to.
#
# Secrets are read from the environment only, never written to disk by this
# service and never logged. Do not commit a filled-in copy of this file.
#
# The prose for each option lives in README.md § "Settings and defaults"; this
# file is the checklist. `test/config/documented.test.ts` fails the build if a
# knob exists in the code and not in the README.

# ─── network and identity ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# REQUIRED. bitcoin | signet | mutinynet | regtest
SWAP_NETWORK=regtest

# REQUIRED. The Arkade wallet. Secret.
ARK_MNEMONIC=

# REQUIRED. arkd endpoint. Startup refuses a server whose reported network
# disagrees with SWAP_NETWORK, rather than quoting against the wrong chain.
ARK_SERVER_URL=

# Covenant co-signer, required on EVERY network — startup throws without it.
# No default ships: this service co-signs the spends that move money, so a URL
# baked into the repository would point a deployment's money path at whichever
# operator that default happened to name.
EMULATOR_URL=

# covclaimd (non-interactive claim daemon): receive legs reveal funded lockups
# to it, so offline clients get paid. Unset = the client claims its own lockup.
# COVCLAIMD_URL=

# The Arkade wallet's view of the Bitcoin chain, as an Esplora REST base URL.
# Unset takes the SDK's per-network default, which is right for the hosted
# networks and wrong inside a container on regtest, where that default is
# http://localhost:3000/api and resolves to the container itself. The wallet
# degrades quietly when it cannot be reached — it logs the failed chain-tip
# fetch once and leaves block-denominated VTXO expiry unwatched.
#
# NOT the same knob as LND_ESPLORA_URL below, which is the Lightning side's.
# ARK_ESPLORA_URL=

# Shown on the registry card. Cosmetic.
# SOLVER_NAME=

# ─── Lightning backend ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# lnd | fake, or the name of a rail a consumer registered
#   lnd   a real LND node over gRPC
#   fake  forge-and-pay-own-invoices, for regtest E2E. REFUSED on mainnet.
#
# REQUIRED unless all four BTC corridors are disabled, and there is no default.
# A rail is a PAIR — one wallet answers both the Lightning and the onchain port
# — so the onchain corridors take their backend from this value too, and only a
# deployment that switches every one of the four off may leave it unset. That is
# how a solver serving only EVM or asset flow says it needs no BTC backend.
# An unrecognised value throws and names the accepted set rather than falling
# through to one.
LN_BACKEND=lnd

# What to believe as the Arkade server's unilateral exit delay, in seconds,
# INSTEAD of the value it advertises at /v1/info. Unset means believe it.
#
# arkd can advertise a delay longer than the minimum it actually enforces, and
# mainnet does: /v1/info reports 605184 (its "Public unilateral exit"), while
# the delay covenant leaves are checked against is the plain "Unilateral exit",
# 259584. That number is not cosmetic here — every covenant this solver writes
# takes its CSV timelocks from it, and the Lightning receive corridor sizes its
# invoice's final CLTV delta against it. At 605184 that corridor asks for 4074
# blocks and cannot be served at all; at or below 296448s (~3.43 days) it is
# served with every safety gate intact.
#
# On mainnet today the value to set is 260096 — one 512s unit above the real
# floor, which keeps 242 blocks of route-CLTV budget. See docs/runbook.md
# § "The recourse window on mainnet" for the full table and the caveats.
#
# THE TWO DIRECTIONS ARE NOT SYMMETRIC. Too HIGH is merely wasteful. Too LOW
# writes a script the server REJECTS — and it rejects at SPEND, not at funding,
# so `INVALID_VTXO_SCRIPT: exit delay is too short` arrives once there is
# already money in the script. Confirm the server ACCEPTS the script with one
# small real spend before this carries a large one — that is what a
# collaborative claim or refund proves; the CSV leaves are only reachable
# through a unilateral exit, which nothing in src/ performs yet. In-flight swaps are unaffected: each row
# snapshots its own delays at quote time.
# ARK_UNILATERAL_EXIT_DELAY=

# ─── the #69 window on lightning:BTC->arkade:BTC ──────────────────────────────

# Serve the Lightning receive corridor even when the solver's own solo recourse
# opens AFTER the incoming htlc's E. Default false, and false is the safe value.
#
# Turn it on and you accept this: if the Arkade server is gone or censoring for
# longer than its exit delay AND E has passed, a trader can let the htlc fail
# back to its payer for free and only then claim the Arkade payout, taking both
# sides. Bounded by LN_RECEIVE_MAX_SATS, which `bitcoin` therefore REQUIRES you
# to set explicitly alongside this — state the number you are risking.
#
# It exists because on mainnet the strict rule is not a tax, it is a wall. arkd
# reports unilateralExitDelay=605184s (7 days), so the solo leaf opens at 7.05
# days and the corridor would need 4074 blocks of final CLTV — about 28 days of
# a payer's funds. Nothing routes that, so every quote is refused
# `recourse_window_unservable` and the corridor cannot run at all.
# LN_RECEIVE_ACCEPT_UNILATERAL_GAP=false

# ─── LND backend (only when LN_BACKEND=lnd) ───────────────────────────────────

# LND_SOCKET=127.0.0.1:10009
# Exactly one of each pair — inline base64, or a path. Setting both throws.
# LND_CERT=            /  LND_CERT_PATH=
# LND_MACAROON=        /  LND_MACAROON_PATH=
# Esplora endpoint for the onchain corridors on this backend.
# LND_ESPLORA_URL=

# Chain tip source, for a deployment whose arkd advertises BLOCK-typed delays
# (its covenant timelocks mature on height, so the service must read one).
# Defaults to LND_ESPLORA_URL. Unused by a seconds-typed deployment.
# CHAIN_TIP_ESPLORA_URL=

# ─── databases ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# One directory for every database this service opens.
# DB_DIR=.data

# The swap database. On a FRESH install every corridor's tables live in this one
# file. A deployment started before consolidation keeps its five suffixed files
# (`-onchain`, `-receive`, `-onchain-receive`, `-admin`) and nothing moves rows
# between them — see README § Backups for which files to back up.
# SWAP_DB_PATH=<DB_DIR>/swaps.sqlite

# Arkade wallet state. Cache plus state; back it up too, it is cheap.
# ARK_DB_PATH=<DB_DIR>/ark.sqlite

# The fake backend's preimage map. Only read when LN_BACKEND=fake.
# FAKE_LN_STATE_PATH=<DB_DIR>/fake-ln.json

# ─── limits, pricing and exposure ─────────────────────────────────────────────

# Deployment-wide per-swap ceiling. Narrows the network default; cannot widen it.
# MAX_SWAP_SATS=

# Aggregate cap across concurrently-exposed swaps, across ALL corridors.
# MAX_EXPOSED_SATS=<3 x maxSats>

# How long a disabled contract row is kept before it is deleted.
# Two-stage retirement: a finished contract whose script holds nothing is first
# disabled (reversible, still annotates its outputs), then deleted after this
# window. Deleting is what stops the wallet re-syncing it forever.
# CONTRACT_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Per-corridor knobs. <CORRIDOR> is one of:
#   LN_SEND | LN_RECEIVE | ONCHAIN_SEND | ONCHAIN_RECEIVE
#
# <CORRIDOR>_MIN_SATS / <CORRIDOR>_MAX_SATS
#   Narrow the range MAX_SWAP_SATS already produced. NARROWS ONLY — a corridor
#   cannot be widened past the deployment cap by reaching for the finer knob.
# <CORRIDOR>_FEE_BPS          spread in basis points, integer [0, 9999]
# <CORRIDOR>_FEE_FLAT_SATS    flat charge, integer [0, 1000000]. A Lightning
#   corridor charging one cannot publish a registry card and will not bid on
#   open RFQs — the schema carries fee_bps only — rather than advertise a rate
#   it will not honour.
# <CORRIDOR>_ENABLED          `true`/`false` EXACTLY; `FALSE`, `0`, `no` throw.
#   `false` darkens the corridor: never constructed, its pair refused
#   `unsupported_pair` at the ingress. Rows already on disk stay readable and
#   refundable — switching a corridor off never strands a funded lockup.
# <CORRIDOR>_FEE_CAP_SATS     integer [1, 1000000], and the switch that makes
#   the corridor price its own chain cost live (vsize x sats/vbyte) instead of
#   charging <CORRIDOR>_FEE_FLAT_SATS. That flat becomes the fallback for when
#   no estimate is available; this is the ceiling the charge can never exceed,
#   and the number a signed registry card can actually promise. ONCHAIN_SEND
#   and ONCHAIN_RECEIVE only — set on a Lightning corridor it throws rather
#   than parse and change nothing.
# <CORRIDOR>_FEE_MIN_SATS     integer [0, 1000000], default 0. The least the
#   corridor will charge however cheap the chain gets — float, refund risk and
#   attention do not fall to zero because fees did. Separate from FEE_FLAT_SATS
#   on purpose: that one is a guess at cost, this one is a minimum. Set without
#   a cap it throws, because with no cap it would do nothing.
#
# LN_SEND_MAX_SATS=
# LN_SEND_FEE_BPS=
# LN_RECEIVE_ENABLED=false
# ONCHAIN_RECEIVE_FEE_CAP_SATS=
# ONCHAIN_RECEIVE_FEE_MIN_SATS=

# How the sampled sats/vbyte reading behind <CORRIDOR>_FEE_CAP_SATS ages. ONE
# sample serves both onchain corridors; a read is what triggers a refresh, so
# an idle solver polls nothing. REFRESH is when a new fetch starts — the old
# value keeps being served meanwhile, so quoting never blocks. STALE is when
# the value stops being served at all and pricing falls back to the configured
# flat. STALE must exceed REFRESH, or every read past the refresh age is null.
# ONCHAIN_FEE_RATE_REFRESH_MS=60000
# ONCHAIN_FEE_RATE_STALE_MS=900000

# Funding window a Lightning-SEND quote grants, as an upper bound. Integer in
# [60, 7200]; the ceiling is derived from the refund safety margin, so a longer
# window could only produce swaps that refuse themselves.
# LOCKUP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=900

# Swaps driven per Lightning-send sweep — the only sweep with fan-out.
# SWEEP_CONCURRENCY=8

# Route hints the Lightning-SEND leg will not price, as a comma-separated list
# of lowercase hex short_channel_ids (16 hex chars each; anything else throws).
# Unset or empty prices every hint, which is what the corridor did before this
# existed — a non-empty list is its own opt-in.
#
# NO EXAMPLE IS GIVEN ON PURPOSE. An entry is only ever added on authoritative
# confirmation — from the wallet vendor, the node operator named by the hint, or
# the recipient — that the channel cannot route. How a scid LOOKS is not
# confirmation: BOLT #2 requires an unannounced channel's option_scid_alias to
# be unrelated to its real short_channel_id and permits it in BOLT 11 r fields,
# so a block field above the chain tip is not a confirmation height at all (LND
# allocates aliases from heights 16000000-16250000, where such a value is
# evidence FOR a live private channel). Private channels are absent from gossip
# too, so "no edge found" proves nothing.
#
# A WRONG entry is a fund-risk, not a lost swap: a routable channel listed here
# is priced out of a refund deadline a route can still take, which on a rail
# that caps nothing is the double-collect window. See docs/runbook.md.
# LN_SEND_HINT_SCID_DENYLIST=

# Whether the watch loop splits the float into the VTXO pool on its own.
# OPT-IN BECAUSE IT SPENDS. `true`/`false` exactly.
# POOL_AUTO_MINT=false

# ─── transport ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Outbound relay for `cli relay`. Unset means relay mode is unavailable.
# RELAY_URL=wss://...

# nostr | dev        (default: nostr)
# Defaults to the production dialect on purpose: a deployment pointed at a real
# Nostr relay with dev framing connects and then goes silently deaf.
# RELAY_PROTOCOL=nostr

# File the relay heartbeat is written to; the container HEALTHCHECK reads it.
# RELAY_HEALTH_PATH=

# off | manual | auto        (default: off)
# Whether this solver advertises itself on Nostr. Under `off` the console's
# "post now" action is REFUSED, not merely unused.
# NOSTR_AD_PUBLISH=off

# Relays listed on the registry card, comma-separated.
# SOLVER_CARD_RELAYS=

# Bids per minute this solver will answer on open RFQs.
# OPEN_RFQ_MAX_BIDS_PER_MIN=

# ─── HTTP ingress (`cli serve`) ───────────────────────────────────────────────

# PORT=8787
# HOST=127.0.0.1

# ─── admin console ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Unset means NO console and no extra socket. Integer [1, 65535]; a bad value
# throws rather than reading as "off".
# ADMIN_PORT=8788

# The bind, and the WHOLE of the console's access control: it has NO
# authentication, so anything that can reach it can move money. 0.0.0.0
# publishes a money-moving interface on every interface the host has — do that
# only behind something that authenticates.
# ADMIN_HOST=127.0.0.1

# ─── EVM corridors (ethereum:<token>) ────────────────────────────────────────

# ALL OFF unless EVM_TOKENS names a token. Nothing below is read otherwise: no
# EVM store is opened, no EVM corridor is constructed, and both EVM pairs are
# refused unsupported_pair at the ingress. Nothing here has a default — a
# default block cadence or acceptance depth is a guess about a chain this
# service has never seen, and both are wrong in a direction that costs money.

# The tokens served: SYMBOL:0xaddress:decimals, comma-separated. The symbol is
# what every knob below names, because EVM_SEND_0XA0B8…_MAX_SATS is legal shell
# and unreadable. A repeated symbol or address throws.
# EVM_TOKENS=USDC:0xa0b86991c6218b36c1d19d4a2e9eb0ce3606eb48:6

# Absent EVM_RPC_URL means the corridor is not served, and is not an error.
# Anything else missing while it IS served is, because the missing half is
# always a safety knob.
# EVM_RPC_URL=
# EVM_HTLC_ADDRESS=
# EVM_CHAIN_ID=

# 32 bytes of hex. SEPARATE from ARK_MNEMONIC on purpose: rotating one must not
# silently rotate the other, including the one holding funds mid-swap.
# EVM_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Both halves of the finality policy, and both are required. Depth alone is not
# finality: a rollup sequencer issues a receipt in 1-2 seconds, so a lock can be
# many confirmations deep and still vanish. The observer takes the later.
# EVM_MIN_CONFIRMATIONS=12
# EVM_MIN_AGE_SECONDS=720
#
# NEEDS A NODE THAT STILL HAS THE BLOCK. Depth is proven by asking the contract
# whether the lock was already there EVM_MIN_CONFIRMATIONS blocks ago, which is
# an eth_call at a historical height. A node that has pruned that far cannot
# answer, so the depth you may configure is bounded by your node state
# retention — the corridor waits rather than advancing on an unproven depth,
# and logs why. Comfortable for L2 cadences; an archive node lifts the bound.

# Read someone else's deadline against the FASTEST plausible cadence, size your
# own against the SLOWEST. A floor above the ceiling throws at startup.
# EVM_FASTEST_SECONDS_PER_BLOCK=0.25
# EVM_SLOWEST_SECONDS_PER_BLOCK=15

# What one transaction may cost, and how long it must stay viable unmined.
# EVM_GAS_LIMIT is a CEILING, not an estimate: eth_estimateGas cannot be trusted
# for a call that reverts under conditions it does not reproduce, and a claim
# that runs out of gas past the timeout is a total loss.
# EVM_GAS_LIMIT=300000
# EVM_MAX_FEE_PER_GAS_CEILING=
# EVM_FEE_HEADROOM_SECONDS=600

# The recourse margin between the two legs, and the least time a client gets to
# claim. On a corridor where they may have to acquire gas first, minutes are thin.
# EVM_ORDER_MARGIN_SECONDS=7200
# EVM_MIN_CLAIM_WINDOW_SECONDS=1800

# How long a quote binds the solver to the quoted rate, seconds. rfq-protocol.md
# §5 puts cross-asset windows "on the order of ~30 seconds" — every second past
# the quote is an unhedged option on a live pair that the spread does not price.
# Funding observed after this is refused and refunded, never filled at stale terms.
# EVM_QUOTE_VALIDITY_SECONDS=60

# Per token and direction, the same family as the <CORRIDOR>_ knobs above.
# EVM_SEND_USDC_ENABLED=true
# EVM_SEND_USDC_MIN_SATS=
# EVM_SEND_USDC_MAX_SATS=
# EVM_SEND_USDC_FEE_BPS=
# EVM_SEND_USDC_MIN_UNITS=
# EVM_SEND_USDC_MAX_UNITS=
# EVM_RECEIVE_USDC_ENABLED=true
# EVM_RECEIVE_USDC_MIN_UNITS=
# EVM_RECEIVE_USDC_MAX_UNITS=

# The token MARKET — no direction in the name, because a market is the pair and
# both directions share it.
# EVM_USDC_PRICE_FEED=
# EVM_USDC_PRICE_PATH=

# ─── Arkade asset offers (the packet path) ───────────────────────────────────

# ALL OFF unless OFFER_MARKETS names a market. Nothing below is read otherwise:
# no offer_fill table is opened, no subscription to arkd's filtered transaction
# stream, nothing decided and nothing spent.
#
# NOT a corridor, so there is no _ENABLED knob: both legs are on Arkade and the
# maker's covenant obliges the fill to pay them in the same transaction, so
# there is no HTLC, no deadline and no refund.
#
# THE SOLVER IS ALWAYS THE TAKER. There is no knob for publishing an offer, and
# that is deliberate — an offer is a standing commitment with no expiry, so
# publishing one writes a free option against this deployment's float.

# A/B pairs, comma-separated. BTC is the sats leg; anything else is a 68-hex
# asset id. Unordered, so one entry serves both directions.
# OFFER_MARKETS=BTC/41bcbb06921a0e9f6fe4f1b003b878cbb43d9ca3f6d14cab7940090458765a390000

# Required once OFFER_MARKETS names a market, with no default: this is how much
# of the float ONE discovered offer may take, which is the deployment's answer
# rather than this repository's. Whole numbers in the WANT leg's own units —
# asset units, or sats when that leg is BTC. A max below the min throws at
# startup, since it would refuse every offer while looking like a quiet market.
# OFFER_MIN_FILL_AMOUNT=
# OFFER_MAX_FILL_AMOUNT=

# ─── testing only ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Mnemonic for the counterparty wallet in E2E runs. Never set in production.
# PAYEE_MNEMONIC=
